[#:seon.task{:name "the-failure-probe-is-declared-program-not-an-interned-host-var"
             :plan "Round 8's landing of merge task 57ad6cd33297 was refused because `seon.test-failure-facts-test/failure-readers-use-the-structured-claims` is red interpreted on unchanged main (non-root `my.test/check`, run 478848ad5219, 3 failures) and green compiled. Cause, measured: its fixture `seon.test-failure-facts-test/with-probe` makes the probe test with `create-ns`, `intern` and `alter-meta!` (a site of `seon.test-census-test/no-test-redefines-a-var`). Interpreted, those act on the agent's SCI image; `seon.test/run` then resolves the probe through `seon.test/resolve-test` in the JVM image, where it does not exist, so the run records no failure claims and the readers see none. The mismatch is the fixture's, not the interpreter's: a test that builds a host Var is not a world value. Its twin `seon.problems-test/a-deleted-function-var-is-derived-from-the-loaded-image` had the same cause (`eval` of a `defn` into the loaded namespace) and was rewritten to retract the function's entity instead. Fix: `seon.test-failure-facts-test/with-probe` declares the probe namespace and test as program rows only (as `seon.test-failure-facts-test/redeclare-probe!` does) and switches mode by redeclaring the probe's source, so `seon.test/run` interprets the same probe under either evaluator; the eleven tests using it assert what an interpreted probe records (no reported file, as `seon.test-failure-facts-test/an-interpreted-test-failure-records-no-false-site` does) or move their site assertions to a declared file test. Done when this proof is green interpreted and compiled. Read `seon.problems/problems`."}]
